course: everyday
lesson: 391
title: «Just hit him back» or «tell a teacher»? What the grown-ups say
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: When the Station Road boys kept knocking Sam's bag off his shoulder, every grown-up in his life gave him a different rule. Grandad said «just hit him back, lad, that's what I did in '72». Sam's teacher said «tell me and I'll sort it». Sam lay in bed doing sums with these rules, like a recipe book where every page says something else about the same cake.
explain: Jake's dad told him «stand up for yourself with your fists», and Kieran's uncle said «if you don't hit back you'll be a target forever». Jake tried it and got a black eye, a detention and a letter home — and the boy he hit came back the next week with his big brother. Kieran's «target forever» turned into «fighting forever». Then Mr Pryce, the head teacher, told Jake: «when you want to hit, come and tell me instead.» The first time Jake did it, he stood in the office doorway with his fists still clenched, and Mr Pryce sorted the problem before lunch.
explain: The girls got muddled rules too. After Connor punched Maddie's nose, her dad, still angry, said «next time, you hit him first». Her mum said «use your words and tell a teacher». Lily's dad said «you're a girl, you should never hit» — as if boys should — and Lily's gran said «ignore them and they'll stop», but the ponytail tugging did not stop, because ignoring only works when the other person is bored, not when they are enjoying it.
explain: Grown-ups give these rules out of love and from their own childhood, and some are half right. But hitting back usually makes a fight bigger and longer, and hitting first is just starting a fight. Telling a trusted adult — and telling again, or another adult, if the first one does nothing — is the rule that works best, for boys and girls alike. Sam told Grandad that, and Grandad, after a long sip of tea, said: «Well. In '72 I also lost a tooth.»
fact: Grown-ups often give children different rules about fighting, usually from their own childhood and out of love.
fact: Hitting back usually makes a fight longer and bigger, and can bring brothers, friends or the whole street into it.
fact: Hitting someone first is starting a fight, even if you are afraid they will hit you.
fact: At school, a child who hits, even to hit back, can get a detention or be sent home.
fact: Telling a trusted adult is not weakness; it brings in someone with the power to stop the trouble.
fact: If the first adult does nothing, it is right to tell them again or tell another adult.
fact: Ignoring teasing works sometimes, but not when the person keeps doing it because they enjoy it.
fact: The rule «do not hurt people» is the same for boys and girls.
fact: Walking away and getting help are not losing; they stop the fight from happening.
fact: Grown-ups can be wrong; you can listen to them kindly and still choose the safer way.
exercise: What did Grandad tell Sam to do? | Just hit the boy back | Tell a teacher straight away | Walk the long way round | Ignore the boys completely
exercise: What did Sam's teacher say? | «Tell me and I'll sort it» | «Sort it out yourselves, you're old enough» | «Hit him back next time, hard» | «Boys will be boys, just ignore it»
exercise: What happened after Jake used his fists as his dad said? | A black eye, a detention, and the boy came back with his brother | The other boy never bothered him again after that day | Everyone at school cheered for him | Nothing happened at all
exercise: Why did Kieran's «target forever» advice go wrong? | Hitting back turned into fighting forever | Kieran never even got the chance to hit anyone back | The uncle took it all back | The other street moved away
exercise: What did Mr Pryce ask Jake to do instead of hitting? | Come and tell him when he wants to hit | Run five laps of the field at break | Write lines in the office | Hit a pillow at home
exercise: How did Jake feel the first time he told Mr Pryce instead of hitting? | Odd, with his fists still clenched, but it worked | Happy and calm from the very start | Scared that Mr Pryce was going to shout at him all afternoon | Bored, because nothing was wrong
exercise: Maddie's dad said «next time, hit him first». Why was that unwise? | Hitting first is starting a fight | Connor was much too fast for her to hit | Girls are not strong enough to hit hard | It would be fine as long as nobody saw it
exercise: What was wrong with Lily's dad's rule «you're a girl, you should never hit»? | Not hurting people is the rule for everyone, not only girls | Girls should hit just as much as boys do | It is fine for girls to hit sometimes, if they are really provoked | Dads should never give rules
exercise: Why did «ignore them» not stop the ponytail tugging? | The boys were enjoying it, so they did not get bored | Lily did not really ignore them at all | Ponytails are too easy to grab | Gran's advice is always wrong, every single time
exercise: Which grown-up's rule works best? | Tell a trusted adult, and tell again if needed | Hit back hard so they learn not to | Hit first so they cannot hit you | Never say anything to anyone about it, ever
exercise: Where do grown-ups usually get their fighting rules from? | Their own childhood, given out of love | A rule book sent out to families by the government | The school's written policy | Films about superheroes
exercise: Sam told a teacher and the teacher forgot. What is wise now? | Tell again, or tell another adult | Give up and hit back next time, harder | Never tell anyone again about anything | Fight the boys after school
exercise: How might Sam feel with all these different rules? | Muddled and unsure whose rule to follow | Sure that Grandad is always right about everything | Happy to have so many choices | Angry at the teacher only
exercise: What did Grandad admit about '72 in the end? | He also lost a tooth | He won every single fight | He never fought at all | He told a teacher instead
exercise: Which is another kind of courage, not fists? | Telling an adult the real reason you are scared | Hitting someone much bigger than you in front of a crowd | Starting the fight first | Shouting louder than everyone
exercise: Maddie's mum said «use your words». What could Maddie say to Connor? | «Don't touch me. I'm telling Mrs Okafor» | «Wait till my dad gets you, then you'll see» | «You're dead after school» | Nothing, just glare at him
test: «my grandad says just deck him, sorted». Is it sorted? | No, hitting back usually makes it bigger and longer | Yes, one good punch ends every fight for ever | Yes, if your grandad says so | Only if you're bigger than him
test: Jake's mate: «u told Pryce?? grass». What's true? | Telling to stop trouble is not grassing | Telling is always grassing, whatever the reason | Only girls tell teachers | Grassing is fine if you're scared
test: «hit first so he can't hit u, simple». What's wrong with that? | Hitting first is starting the fight yourself | Nothing, it's clever, you get the first hit in | It only works on small kids | It's fine if he looked at you funny
test: Lily's gran: «ignore them, love, they'll stop». Two weeks on, still tugging. Now what? | Tell a teacher, because ignoring hasn't worked | Keep ignoring them for a whole year if she has to | Tug their hair back | Cut her ponytail off
test: Kieran: «if ya don't hit back you're a target forever, my uncle said». Which is true? | Hitting back often keeps the fight going for longer | Nobody ever picks on people who hit back | Uncles are always right about fights | Targets get a medal
test: teacher on duty, busy: «not now, sort it yourselves». What next? | Find another adult and tell them | Sort it out with fists | Go home and never come back to school | Cry and do nothing
test: «girls don't hit, but boys gotta, innit». Right? | No, the rule about not hurting is the same for all | Yes, boys must fight or they're not proper boys | Yes, girls are too weak | Only on Fridays
test: Mr Pryce: «Jake, why are you in my doorway with your fists clenched?» Best answer? | «I wanted to hit him, so I came to tell you» | «Nothing, sir, I'm just standing here» | «I'm practising my boxing for the club on Saturday» | «Waiting to hit him after school»
test: Sam: «Grandad's old so he must know best?» Best reply? | Grown-ups can be wrong; listen kindly, choose the safer way | Old people are always right about everything | Old people are always wrong | Do what the oldest person in the family says
test: Maddie's dad, still angry about her nose: «next time you hit him first!» Wise Maddie says? | «I'd rather tell a teacher, Dad, so it ends» | «OK Dad, I'll punch him first thing on Monday» | «I'll get Kieran to do it» | «I'll hit him twice»
test: little brother: «so what DO I do if someone shoves me?» Best answer? | Step away, then tell a grown-up you trust | Shove back harder than they shoved you | Hit the nearest person | Keep it a secret from everyone
exam: Identify the principal reason for which the instruction «retaliate with physical force» is considered inadvisable for a pupil subjected to provocation. | Retaliation customarily prolongs and enlarges the conflict | Retaliation is permissible solely outside the school premises | Retaliation is ineffective solely on account of the pupil's size | Retaliation is inadvisable only for female pupils and younger children
exam: Whereas a pupil has reported mistreatment to a member of staff who has taken no action, select the response most consistent with established guidance. | The report ought to be repeated, or made to another responsible adult | The matter ought thereafter to be resolved by force between the pupils | No further report ought to be made | The pupil ought to absent himself from school
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the striking of the first blow in anticipation of an attack? | It constitutes the initiation of a fight | It constitutes lawful self-defence in all cases | It constitutes the avoidance of a fight altogether | It is without consequence where no injury results
exam: State the conclusion consistent with the established facts concerning the rule against inflicting harm upon others. | The rule applies equally irrespective of the sex of the pupil | The rule applies solely to female pupils | The rule applies solely within the classroom and not at break | The rule is suspended where an adult so directs
